That’s a solid direction. Here’s a concise, practical blueprint to merge telemetry and programs into a single management ecosystem with real-time web display.

High-level architecture
- Agents and/or agentless data sources
  - Deploy lightweight cross-platform agents on each node (Windows/Linux/macOS, containerized as needed).
  - Agent responsibilities: collect telemetry, health/status, inventory, events, and commands for orchestration.
  - Optional agentless paths for read-only data sources (SNMP, SSH, REST endpoints) if needed.
- Ingestion layer
  - Use a scalable message bus or stream: Kafka or NATS for high-throughput, durable transport.
  - Normalize data with a common schema (telemetry, metrics, logs, events) before persisting.
- Processing and storage
  - Time-series storage for metrics: TimescaleDB, InfluxDB.
  - Event/log storage: Elasticsearch or OpenSearch; optionally a log pipeline (Fluent Bit / Logstash).
  - Metadata/catalog: a central registry for agents, devices, licenses, and configurations (PostgreSQL or a lightweight graph DB).
- Real-time processing
  - Stream processing: Kafka Streams, Apache Flink, or lightweight processors to compute health, baselines, and alerts.
- Visualization and control plane
  - Web frontend: real-time dashboards (Grafana or a custom UI) embedded in your web agent system.
  - Real-time updates: WebSocket or Server-Sent Events (SSE) for live telemetry on the web UI.
  - Orchestration controls: push configurations, deploy updates, and run ad-hoc commands to agents.
- Deployment and management
  - Containerize the management system components (Docker/Kubernetes) for reproducibility and scale.
  - Agent deployment can be via lightweight installers, container images, or configuration management tools (Ansible, Puppet, Salt).
- Security and governance
  - Mutual TLS, strong authentication (mTLS or OAuth2), per-agent RBAC.
  - Signed configurations/commands, audit logs, and role-based access control.
  - Data minimization and encryption at rest/in transit.
- Reliability and observability
  - Redundancy for core services, health checks, circuit breakers.
  - Centralized logging, tracing, and metrics for the control plane.

Recommended practical stack (example)
- Ingestion: Kafka or NATS
- Processing: Flink or Kafka Streams
- Storage: TimescaleDB (metrics), OpenSearch (logs), PostgreSQL (metadata)
- Visualization: Grafana (dashboards), or a custom React/Next.js app with WebSocket
- Agents: cross-platform Go or Rust agents; Dockerized for cloud/on-prem
- Security: mTLS, OAuth2/OIDC, ROT(k) for secrets, Vault for key management

Implementation plan (MVP)
1) Define data contracts
   - Telemetry schema: host_id, timestamp, metric_name, value, unit, source, severity
   - Inventory: hostname, OS, agent_version, tags, capabilities
   - Events/logs: event_type, message, severity, timestamp, correlated_id
2) Build a minimal agent
   - Collect a small set of metrics (CPU, memory, disk, network) + heartbeat
   - Send to a local bridge that fronts the ingestion layer
3) Set up ingestion and storage
   - Deploy Kafka cluster or a managed alternative
   - Create topics for metrics, logs, and events
   - Persist metrics to TimescaleDB, logs to OpenSearch
4) Real-time UI
   - Quick dashboard with live node health and top metrics
   - WebSocket feed for live updates
5) Basic orchestration
   - Ability to push a config snippet or run a simple command on agents
6) Security and access
   - Enforce TLS, authenticate agents, implement basic RBAC
7) Extendability
   - Add more data sources, richer telemetry, and additional visualization panels

Key questions to tailor your plan
- Scope: how many nodes and what OSes are you targeting?
- Data volume and retention: how much telemetry, and for how long?
- Network topology: on-prem, cloud, or hybrid? Any firewalls/proxies to consider?
- Security posture: required auth method, compliance needs?
- Preferred tech stack or constraints (languages, existing systems)?

